        "#| code-fold: true\n",
        "#| code-summary: \"Libraries\"\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import missingno as msno\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
        "import seaborn as sns\n",
        "\n",
        "sns.set_style('whitegrid')\n",
        "sns.set_palette('Set2')"
      ],
      "id": "bf479e7b",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Tidy Data and Common Problems\n",
        "\n",
        "In the previous chapter we introduced the **tidy data standard**:\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-important title=\"Tidy Data Standard\"}\n",
        "For data to be tidy it must satisfy the following three rules:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Each variable is a column.\n",
        "2. Each observation is a row.\n",
        "3. Each type of observational unit forms a table.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "In practice, raw data almost never arrives in this form. Beyond structure, real datasets routinely contain:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Missing values** — entries that were never collected, lost, or recorded incorrectly.\n",
        "- **Duplicate records** — rows that represent the same observation more than once.\n",
        "- **Inconsistent or incorrect data types** — dates stored as strings, numbers encoded as text.\n",
        "- **Invalid values** — entries that appear real but break domain rules (e.g. a negative age).\n",
        "- **Labelling issues** — the same category written in multiple ways across rows or files.\n",
        "\n",
        "Data preparation is the process of systematically identifying and resolving these problems before analysis.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-important title=\"Data Preparation\"}\n",
        "**Data preparation** is the process of cleaning and transforming data to make it suitable for analysis.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "The workflow for each problem follows the same pattern:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Identify** — find what is wrong with the data.\n",
        "2. **Determine** — decide how to fix the problem.\n",
        "3. **Update and check** — apply the fix and verify the result.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Missing Data\n",
        "\n",
        "### Causes and consequences\n",
        "\n",
        "Missing data can arise for many reasons: a field was never collected, data was collected but subsequently lost, or a value was recorded incorrectly and then nullified. Understanding *why* data is missing is important because it determines what treatment is appropriate.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Classifying missingness\n",
        "\n",
        "There are three standard classifications:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Missing Completely at Random (MCAR)** — missingness is independent of both observed and unobserved data. Deleting missing rows introduces no bias.\n",
        "2. **Missing at Random (MAR)** — missingness can be fully accounted for by other observed variables. Imputation using those variables is justified.\n",
        "3. **Missing Not at Random (MNAR)** — missingness depends on the missing value itself or on unobserved factors. This is the most problematic case and requires careful modelling.\n",
        "\n",
        "Missingness is often informative in its own right. A blank income field may mean the respondent preferred not to disclose it (biasing a sample toward lower earners). Missing follow-up appointments in a clinical trial may indicate patients who became too ill to return (biasing an efficacy estimate upward). Ignoring these patterns leads to biased analysis.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Practice data\n",
        "\n",
        "We will use a synthetic student academic records dataset throughout this section. It has enough columns and varied missingness patterns to illustrate the key tools."
      ],
      "id": "e773f4bd"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| code-fold: true\n",
        "#| code-summary: \"Creating synthetic student dataset with missing values.\"\n",
        "rng = np.random.default_rng(42)\n",
        "n = 500\n",
        "\n",
        "year  = rng.choice([1, 2, 3, 4], size=n, p=[0.25, 0.25, 0.25, 0.25])\n",
        "gpa   = rng.normal(3.0, 0.5, size=n).clip(0, 4.0)\n",
        "\n",
        "# SAT score — missing for ~15% (some students sat the ACT instead)\n",
        "sat = rng.integers(900, 1600, size=n).astype(float)\n",
        "sat[rng.random(n) < 0.15] = np.nan\n",
        "\n",
        "# Income bracket and parent education — sensitive fields, often missing together\n",
        "income_mask   = rng.random(n) < 0.30\n",
        "parent_mask   = income_mask | (rng.random(n) < 0.10)\n",
        "income        = pd.Series(rng.choice([\"Low\", \"Middle\", \"High\"], size=n), dtype=object)\n",
        "parent_ed     = pd.Series(rng.choice([\"High School\", \"Some College\", \"Bachelor's\", \"Graduate\"], size=n), dtype=object)\n",
        "income[income_mask]     = np.nan\n",
        "parent_ed[parent_mask]  = np.nan\n",
        "\n",
        "study_hours   = rng.integers(5, 40, size=n).astype(float)\n",
        "study_hours[rng.random(n) < 0.10] = np.nan\n",
        "\n",
        "internship    = rng.choice([0, 1], size=n).astype(float)\n",
        "internship[rng.random(n) < 0.25] = np.nan\n",
        "\n",
        "# Research hours — mostly missing for first- and second-year students\n",
        "research      = rng.integers(0, 20, size=n).astype(float)\n",
        "research[(year < 3) | (rng.random(n) < 0.30)] = np.nan\n",
        "\n",
        "# Scholarship and financial aid — correlated missingness\n",
        "scholarship_mask  = rng.random(n) < 0.20\n",
        "scholarship       = rng.choice([0, 1], size=n).astype(float)\n",
        "financial_aid     = rng.integers(0, 20000, size=n).astype(float)\n",
        "scholarship[scholarship_mask] = np.nan\n",
        "financial_aid[scholarship_mask | (rng.random(n) < 0.10)] = np.nan\n",
        "\n",
        "# Thesis score — only exists for year-4 students\n",
        "thesis = rng.normal(75, 10, size=n).clip(0, 100)\n",
        "thesis[year < 4] = np.nan\n",
        "\n",
        "mentor         = rng.choice([0, 1], size=n).astype(float)\n",
        "mentor[rng.random(n) < 0.05] = np.nan\n",
        "\n",
        "# Exit survey — only year-4 students, and only ~40% completed it\n",
        "exit_survey = rng.integers(1, 10, size=n).astype(float)\n",
        "exit_survey[(year < 4) | (rng.random(n) < 0.60)] = np.nan\n",
        "\n",
        "students = pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    'student_id':    [f\"S{str(i).zfill(4)}\" for i in range(1, n + 1)],\n",
        "    'year':          year,\n",
        "    'gpa':           gpa,\n",
        "    'sat_score':     sat,\n",
        "    'income_bracket': income,\n",
        "    'parent_education': parent_ed,\n",
        "    'study_hours_wk': study_hours,\n",
        "    'internship':    internship,\n",
        "    'research_hours': research,\n",
        "    'scholarship':   scholarship,\n",
        "    'financial_aid': financial_aid,\n",
        "    'thesis_score':  thesis,\n",
        "    'mentor_assigned': mentor,\n",
        "    'exit_survey_score': exit_survey,\n",
        "})"
      ],
      "id": "d28d2299",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Inspecting missingness\n",
        "\n",
        "The first step is to understand the scale of the problem."
      ],
      "id": "6d7239b8"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "students.head()"
      ],
      "id": "80472851",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "students.shape"
      ],
      "id": "92f722b6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We count missing values per column and compute an overall percentage."
      ],
      "id": "e2b2c2f7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "missing_counts = students.isnull().sum().sort_values(ascending=False)\n",
        "missing_counts"
      ],
      "id": "fe565b35",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "total_cells   = np.prod(students.shape)\n",
        "total_missing = missing_counts.sum()\n",
        "print(f\"Overall missingness: {total_missing / total_cells * 100:.1f}%\")"
      ],
      "id": "e7bedca0",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Visualising missingness with `missingno`\n",
        "\n",
        "The `missingno` library provides four complementary views of missing data patterns.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Bar chart** — shows the count of non-null values per column at a glance."
      ],
      "id": "ac425771"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| fig-cap: \"Non-null value counts per column (bar chart).\"\n",
        "msno.bar(students, figsize=(9, 5))\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "64b1aff5",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Matrix** — plots a random sample of rows; white streaks mark missing entries. Aligned streaks across columns indicate that variables are missing together."
      ],
      "id": "6112448d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| fig-cap: \"Missing data matrix for 200 randomly sampled rows.\"\n",
        "msno.matrix(students.sample(200, random_state=1), figsize=(11, 4))\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "064d0fae",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Heatmap** — shows the correlation between missingness indicators across pairs of columns. A positive value means the two columns tend to be missing at the same time; a negative value means they are missing at opposite times."
      ],
      "id": "a5671d54"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| fig-cap: \"Missingness correlation heatmap.\"\n",
        "msno.heatmap(students, figsize=(10, 4))\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "1cfe4665",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Dendrogram** — clusters variables by the similarity of their missingness patterns. Variables linked at distance 0 fully predict one another's presence."
      ],
      "id": "c9499c29"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| fig-cap: \"Missingness dendrogram.\"\n",
        "msno.dendrogram(students, figsize=(10, 4))\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "f3f6d82f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "From these plots we can see that `scholarship` and `financial_aid` have highly correlated missingness (they were missing together by design), while `thesis_score` and `exit_survey_score` are missing as a structural consequence of student year — a clear MNAR pattern.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Handling missing data\n",
        "\n",
        "**Deletion** is appropriate when data is MCAR. It is simple but loses information."
      ],
      "id": "77ef2dee"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Drop rows with any missing value\n",
        "students_row_dropped = students.dropna()\n",
        "print(f\"Original rows:       {students.shape[0]}\")\n",
        "print(f\"After row deletion:  {students_row_dropped.shape[0]}\")"
      ],
      "id": "0b5cf914",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Drop columns with any missing value\n",
        "students_col_dropped = students.dropna(axis=1)\n",
        "print(f\"Original columns:    {students.shape[1]}\")\n",
        "print(f\"After col deletion:  {students_col_dropped.shape[1]}\")"
      ],
      "id": "7528a362",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Row deletion eliminates almost every record here because nearly every row has at least one missing field. Column deletion removes any column with even one null. In practice, targeted deletion — dropping only columns or rows above a missingness threshold — is more useful.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Imputation** replaces missing values with plausible substitutes. The `fillna` method covers the most common strategies:"
      ],
      "id": "d5b897bf"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Mean imputation for study_hours_wk (MCAR — plausibly random non-response)\n",
        "students['study_hours_wk'] = students['study_hours_wk'].fillna(\n",
        "    students['study_hours_wk'].mean()\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "# Forward fill for a time-ordered column (not this dataset, but common in time-series)\n",
        "# df['col'].ffill()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Constant fill for remaining numeric columns as a simple baseline\n",
        "students_imputed = students.bfill(axis=0).fillna(0)\n",
        "students_imputed.isnull().sum().sum()"
      ],
      "id": "5de61b78",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {.callout-important title=\"Important\"}\n",
        "Imputation is a deep topic in its own right. The right strategy depends on why data is missing and on the downstream analysis. Always ask: *would imputing this value introduce bias?*\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Duplicate Values\n",
        "\n",
        "### Why duplicates arise\n",
        "\n",
        "Well-structured data should have a unique identifier for each observation. Duplicates typically arise from data entry errors, merging datasets without deduplication, or system migrations.\n",
        "\n",
        "We create a small customer dataset that contains both exact duplicates and near-duplicates arising from inconsistent capitalisation."
      ],
      "id": "0f641f32"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "customers = pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    'customer_id': [1001, 1002, 1003, 1004, 1005, 1003, 1006, 1002, 1007, 1004, 1008],\n",
        "    'name':        ['Alice Johnson', 'bob smith',   'Carol White', 'david lee',\n",
        "                    'Eve Martinez',  'Carol White', 'Frank Brown', 'Bob Smith',\n",
        "                    'Grace Kim',     'David Lee',   'Hank Wilson'],\n",
        "    'email':       ['alice@example.com', 'bob@example.com',   'carol@example.com',\n",
        "                    'david@example.com', 'eve@example.com',   'carol@example.com',\n",
        "                    'frank@example.com', 'bob@example.com',   'grace@example.com',\n",
        "                    'david@example.com', 'hank@example.com'],\n",
        "    'age':         [28, 34, 45, 29, 52, 45, 38, 34, 41, 29, 55],\n",
        "    'city':        ['New York', 'los angeles', 'Chicago',     'new york', 'Houston',\n",
        "                    'Chicago',  'Phoenix',     'Los Angeles', 'Seattle',  'New York', 'Dallas'],\n",
        "    'spend':       [120.50, 89.99, 245.00, 55.75, 310.20,\n",
        "                    245.00, 178.40, 89.99,  95.60, 120.00, 430.10],\n",
        "})\n",
        "customers"
      ],
      "id": "124548d8",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Identifying exact duplicates"
      ],
      "id": "b1b727b9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "dup_count = customers.duplicated().sum()\n",
        "dup_rows  = customers.loc[customers.duplicated()].index.tolist()\n",
        "print(f\"Exact duplicates: {dup_count}  (rows {dup_rows})\")"
      ],
      "id": "a411840d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Near-duplicates from formatting inconsistency\n",
        "\n",
        "Exact duplicate detection misses rows that differ only in case or whitespace — `\"bob smith\"` and `\"Bob Smith\"` are the same person. Normalising strings first reveals the full extent of the problem."
      ],
      "id": "a16c1088"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "customers['name']  = customers['name'].str.strip().str.title()\n",
        "customers['email'] = customers['email'].str.strip().str.lower()\n",
        "customers['city']  = customers['city'].str.strip().str.title()\n",
        "\n",
        "dup_count = customers.duplicated().sum()\n",
        "dup_rows  = customers.loc[customers.duplicated()].index.tolist()\n",
        "print(f\"Duplicates after normalisation: {dup_count}  (rows {dup_rows})\")"
      ],
      "id": "de129332",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Removing duplicates\n",
        "\n",
        "The simplest resolution is deletion — keep the first occurrence."
      ],
      "id": "caacb71a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "customers_clean = customers.drop_duplicates()\n",
        "print(f\"Rows after deduplication: {len(customers_clean)}\")"
      ],
      "id": "529828af",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "When we want to maximise information, we can **aggregate** rather than delete. Here we sum spend across duplicate customer IDs and take the first value for all other fields."
      ],
      "id": "bc042a3e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "customers_agg = (\n",
        "    customers\n",
        "    .groupby('customer_id', sort=False)\n",
        "    .agg({'name': 'first', 'email': 'first', 'age': 'mean', 'city': 'first', 'spend': 'sum'})\n",
        "    .reset_index()\n",
        ")\n",
        "customers_agg"
      ],
      "id": "c9ddcce9",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Inconsistent Data Types\n",
        "\n",
        "Pandas infers column types when loading a file, but inference is not always correct. Wrong types break operations silently — averaging a column of numbers stored as strings produces an error, and comparing dates stored as text gives alphabetical rather than chronological ordering. The goal is for every column to have a type that matches its meaning.\n",
        "\n",
        "Useful inspection tools: `dtypes`, `info()`, `head()`, and `unique()`."
      ],
      "id": "0b1da5ed"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "example_df = pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    \"date\":    [\"2024-01-01\", \"2024-01-02\", \"2024-01-03\"],\n",
        "    \"price\":   [\"1.10\", \"2.20\", \"3.30\"],\n",
        "    \"in_stock\": [\"True\", \"False\", \"True\"],\n",
        "})\n",
        "print(example_df.dtypes)"
      ],
      "id": "80a46c2d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "All three columns are read as `object` (string). Each needs to be coerced to its correct type.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Dates stored as strings"
      ],
      "id": "05d2a455"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "print(f\"Before: {example_df['date'].dtype}\")\n",
        "example_df['date'] = pd.to_datetime(example_df['date'])\n",
        "print(f\"After:  {example_df['date'].dtype}\")"
      ],
      "id": "64b1b01a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Numbers stored as strings"
      ],
      "id": "dcb242ca"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "print(f\"Before: {example_df['price'].dtype}\")\n",
        "example_df['price'] = pd.to_numeric(example_df['price'])\n",
        "print(f\"After:  {example_df['price'].dtype}\")"
      ],
      "id": "92493eb0",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Booleans stored as strings"
      ],
      "id": "c5b91bde"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "print(f\"Before: {example_df['in_stock'].dtype}\")\n",
        "example_df['in_stock'] = (\n",
        "    example_df['in_stock'].str.strip().str.lower()\n",
        "    .map({\"true\": True, \"false\": False})\n",
        "    .astype(\"boolean\")\n",
        ")\n",
        "print(f\"After:  {example_df['in_stock'].dtype}\")"
      ],
      "id": "0508c0fa",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Mixed date formats\n",
        "\n",
        "Hand-prepared data often contains dates written in multiple formats within the same column."
      ],
      "id": "8285273d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "mixed_df = pd.DataFrame({\n",
